# ── Line endings for Node script modules ─────────────────────────────────────
#
# The repo uses core.autocrlf=true, so a fresh clone on Windows gets CRLF in the
# working tree. That is harmless for the site, but NOT for the Node ESM scripts
# under scripts/: vitest's transform fails on a CRLF .mjs with a bare
# "SyntaxError: Invalid or unexpected token" and no location — while Node itself
# imports the exact same file without complaint.
#
# Reproduced in the portal (asj-astro) on 2026-09-12: identical content failed
# with CRLF and passed with LF. Pinning these to LF makes it deterministic
# instead of depending on how the working tree happened to be checked out. Git
# already stores them as LF, so this changes nothing about the committed content.
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.cjs text eol=lf

# ── Shell scripts, for a different and sharper reason ────────────────────────
#
# A CRLF shell script is already wrong on principle, but the concrete damage is
# worse than a stray `\r` in a message: helpers that pass multi-line string
# anchors through the ENVIRONMENT and assert "exactly one match" before writing
# miss every anchor in a CRLF working tree. The loud case is a hard ABORT; the
# bad case is a helper that does not assert, where the file is rewritten
# unchanged and the check stays green — indistinguishable from a real hole.
#
# The portal carries this rule for its mutation batteries. This repo has no
# batteries, but the rule is kept: it is the same class of local-only,
# checkout-dependent failure, and it costs nothing to be deterministic.
*.sh text eol=lf
